<h2>The prosecution case and the defence answer</h2>
<p>The FIR, lodged in January 2026 at Birpur police station in Supaul, carried grave allegations: the informant&rsquo;s minor daughter had been kidnapped, the family threatened with a pistol, and — the informant alleged — the accused had intended to commit rape. A Medical Board opinion recorded that rape &ldquo;cannot be ruled out.&rdquo; The provisions invoked spanned the BNS (Sections 96, 140(1), 61(2) and 3(5)) and the POCSO Act (Sections 8, 12 and 17). The defence attacked the case on three fronts: the occurrence allegedly dated to 24 October 2025, yet the FIR came only on 23 January 2026, a delay of about three months without explanation; the victim&rsquo;s own statement under Section 183 BNSS did not support the prosecution; and the petitioner had been in custody since 23 March 2026 with no antecedents.</p>

<h2>The two documents that mattered</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Section 183 BNSS statement.</strong> The successor to Section 164 CrPC, a statement recorded before a Magistrate. Justice Ritesh Kumar recorded that he had gone through it along with the case diary — and it was one of the two bases for admitting the accused to bail.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Medical Board age assessment.</strong> The Board estimated the victim&rsquo;s age at 18 to 20 years. If she was an adult, the POCSO framework — and with it the statutory presumptions — would fall away, leaving the prosecution to prove its BNS counts on ordinary principles.</div>
</div>
<p>The informant&rsquo;s counsel contested reliance on the victim&rsquo;s statement, maintaining that she was a minor. The Court did not resolve that dispute — bail orders do not — but found in the combination of the statement and the age estimate enough to grant release, subject to antecedent verification by the trial court.</p>

<h2>Age disputes at the bail stage: how courts navigate them</h2>
<p>Age is the jurisdictional fact of every POCSO case, and it is rarely settled when bail is argued. The statutory ladder in Section 94 of the Juvenile Justice Act — matriculation certificate, then school or municipal birth records, then medical opinion — operates fully at trial. At the bail stage, courts work with whatever the case diary offers. Where documentary age proof is absent or disputed and a Medical Board places the victim above the majority line, the prima facie weight of the POCSO counts weakens; where school records firmly establish minority, a contrary medical estimate counts for little. The Patna order sits comfortably in that pattern: the age estimate did not decide the case, but it moved the bail calculus.</p>

<h2>The delay factor</h2>
<p>The unexplained gap between occurrence and FIR deserves separate mention. Delay in lodging an FIR is never fatal by itself — in sexual offence cases, courts rightly accept that families hesitate. But an unexplained three-month delay, combined with a victim&rsquo;s statement that does not support the prosecution, changes its complexion: it becomes part of a cumulative picture that the defence is entitled to urge at the bail stage. The order records the submission without endorsing it — the trial will test it.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>For the defence: obtain the Section 183 BNSS statement and the medical age report at the earliest; they are the two most bail-relevant documents in the diary.</p>
  <p>For complainants: where minority is documented, place school or birth records before the bail court — a medical estimate is the weakest rung of the age ladder and can be met with better proof.</p>
  <p>For both sides: a bail order decides custody, not truth; findings recorded in it do not bind the trial.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information only, withholding all victim-identifying details. Nothing here comments on the merits of the pending trial.</div>
